course: dictionary
lesson: 11246
title: Worn: one word, many meanings
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Worn comes from the verb wear, which goes wear, wore, worn. Look at the words around it to find which meaning fits. «She has worn that hat all week» means she has had it on her head, so worn is the past form of wear.
explain: Most of the time worn means old and damaged by use. «Clothes given to clothes banks are sorted: good ones are resold, and worn ones become rags or stuffing.» Worn ones are clothes that are thin and used up. Worn out is stronger: it means so damaged it can no longer be used.
explain: Worn can be a safety word. «Worn brake pads or loose cables mean you can't stop in time.» The clues are stopping and danger, so worn here means damaged in a way that is dangerous. Always swap your meaning into the sentence, like damaged or used up, and check it still makes sense.
explain: Worn can also mean very tired. Ben says, «I'm knackered», and knackered is UK slang for worn out. Worn can describe land too: rocks and coasts are worn away by water and wind over many years.
fact: Worn is the past participle of wear: wear, wore, worn.
fact: Worn means damaged, thin or faded because it has been used a lot, as in worn shoes or a worn carpet.
fact: Worn out means so damaged it can no longer be used, or very tired.
fact: Worn brakes, worn tyres and worn cables are dangerous and must be replaced.
fact: In science, water and wind can wear rocks and coasts away over a long time. This is called weathering and erosion.
exercise: «Clothes given to clothes banks are sorted: good ones are resold, and worn ones become rags or stuffing.» What does worn mean here? | Brand new and shiny | Sold in the shop at full price | *Old and damaged by use | Dry and warm
exercise: «Worn brake pads or loose cables mean you can't stop in time.» Why are worn brakes dangerous? | *They may not stop the car in time | They make the car go faster | They make a loud noise only | They are too shiny to see
exercise: Find the word in «She has worn that hat all week» that is the past form of wear. | she | week | *worn | has
test: Ben says «I'm knackered after the match». What does knackered mean? | *Worn out, very tired | Angry with a friend | Ready for a race | Full of food after dinner
test: What does worn away mean for a coast? | Built up with new sand overnight | *Slowly eaten away by water and wind | Painted bright blue | Moved to a new country
exam: Which phrase means so damaged it can no longer be used? | *Worn out | Wearing a coat | Worn it well | Wore off
